<HelpTab>
    <HelpSection Title="Changing your password">
        <FluentText>
            Under <strong>Security</strong>, select <strong>Change Password</strong>. If you've forgotten it, ask a
            manager to send you a password reset email.
        </FluentText>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="Face ID and fingerprint sign-in">
        <FluentText>
            Sign in with your phone's Face ID, Touch ID or fingerprint instead of typing your password. Set it up
            under <strong>Face ID &amp; Fingerprint Sign-in</strong> on each phone or computer you use, and use
            <strong>Add Another Device</strong> for the next one. Your fingerprint and face never leave your device.
            Lanyard only stores a key that your device unlocks.
        </FluentText>
    </HelpSection>

    <HelpSection Title="Two-factor authentication">
        <FluentText>Two-factor authentication asks for a code as well as your password. Choose one:</FluentText>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Set Up Authenticator App</strong>: scan the QR code with an app such as Google Authenticator or
                Authy, then enter the 6-digit code and select <strong>Verify and Enable</strong>.</li>
            <li><strong>Enable Email Codes</strong>: a code is emailed to you each time you sign in.</li>
        </ul>
        <HelpNote Intent="MessageBarIntent.Warning">
            Keep your recovery codes somewhere safe. They get you in if you lose your phone. Use <strong>Regenerate
            Recovery Codes</strong> if you've used them up or think someone else has seen them.
        </HelpNote>
        <FluentText>
            If your company requires two-factor authentication, you can't turn it off, but you can still switch
            from email codes to an authenticator app.
        </FluentText>
    </HelpSection>
</HelpTab>
